You cut out too much context. When replying, you shouldn't throw away
previous levels of quotes. The whole point of using email for this
interaction is that it's really straightforward: you reply, and it
includes all the previous context. You trim any you don't need - but
it's vertical trimming, not horizontal trimming. In other words, you
don't remove entire indentation levels. When you edit to specific parts
of a conversation, you include each of the levels of context (and you
can elide stuff if it's helpful).

Point being: you're responding to a question I asked, but _I_ don't know
what my question is about, because the original context is lost.

Here's another principle: every email should be readable on its own. It
should have enough context that the material in it makes sense and you
don't have to go hunting for other emails, but not so much that it adds
noise to the conversation.

